title: $:/plugins/nikorion/base-fields/language/en-GB/readme
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

!! Overview

Adds four everyday fields to the tiddler edit template:

|!Field |!Control |!Stored value |
|`nature` |controlled vocabulary, single value |a bare slug (`concept`, `guide`, …) |
|`status` |controlled vocabulary, single value |a bare slug (`stable`, `raw`, …) |
|`project` |free text, with a dropdown of the values already used in the wiki |free text |
|`tools` |list field, edited like tags, on the Tags row |a list of titles |

`nature`, `status` and `project` share the ''Type'' row: the four controls sit on one flex line and shrink evenly — Type included — so the row always fits the tiddler width, wrapping instead of overflowing when the tiddler is narrow. `tools` shares the ''Tags'' row, as a second box of the same kind — the two are strictly independent: the tools dropdown offers the values already used in `tools`, never the wiki's tags, and vice versa. Below 1000px of window width the two boxes stack again.

Nature, Status and Project each carry the same clear button the core puts on Type; it only shows when the field has a value.

!! Controlled vocabularies

Only the slug is ever written to the field. Icons and labels are display-only, resolved at render time, so a wiki stays readable and filterable in any language:

* the //allowed values and their order// live in the `list` field of `vocab/nature` and `vocab/status`
* the //icons// live in `vocab/icons.multids`, keyed `<field>/<value>`
* the //labels// live in `language/<lang>/vocab.multids`, keyed `Vocab/<field>/<value>`

Each value can also carry a one-line description, shown as a tooltip when hovering it in the dropdown: the `Vocab/<field>/<value>/Hint` strings, optional — a value without one simply has no tooltip.

Adding, removing or reordering a value is done in the plugin source, not through the UI — there is no vocabulary editor, on purpose: a value added through the interface could not carry its translations. To add one, append its slug to the relevant `list` field, then add its icon and its label in each language. A value with no label falls back to its own slug; a value with no icon simply renders without one.

Shipped out of the box:

* `nature` — concept, ref, guide, pattern, snippet, template, decision, log, hub, task, tasklist, routine
* `status` — archived, deprecated, stable, raw

Two helpers are exposed globally for use in view templates and lists: `<<bf-vocab-item field value>>` renders icon + label, and the `bf-vocab-label` / `bf-vocab-icon` / `bf-vocab-values` functions give the pieces separately.

!! How it works

The edit template renders each of its sections as a separate block, so putting controls on a shared row takes one of two routes — and this plugin uses each one once.

''Nature, Status and Project'' go inside the Type control itself: `$:/core/ui/EditTemplate/type` is shipped as a verbatim copy of the core tiddler, with the three controls inserted into the existing `tc-edit-type-selector-wrapper`. It is a ''core override'': upgrading TiddlyWiki will not bring in upstream changes to that tiddler until this plugin is updated too.

''Tools'' takes the other route, and overrides nothing. It stays a section of its own (`list-after: $:/core/ui/EditTemplate/tags`), and the stylesheet turns the container the sections already share into a two-column grid: every section spans both columns, except the tags box and the tools box, which take one each. The tags tiddler — the one that does change between TiddlyWiki releases — is left untouched, and nothing conflicts with another plugin styling or overriding it. The trade-off is that the selector leans on the edit form's internal nesting (`.tc-keyboard > .tc-keyboard`): should that ever change, the two boxes simply stack again, which is visible at a glance rather than silently wrong.

Two more things the stylesheet and the markup take care of:

# the stylesheet neutralises the core `min-width` computed by `set-type-selector-min-width`, which otherwise keeps the type control from shrinking
# the tools input drives the core `keyboard-driven-input` macro directly rather than the `tag-picker` macro, which hardcodes the tags placeholder and reuses the tag input state the edit template defines once for the whole form — sharing it would let a half-typed tool name be added as a //tag// by the save keyboard shortcut

!! Requirements

TiddlyWiki >= 5.3.0. No other dependency.

